WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.913 --> 00:00:15.110
I am a neurosurgeon,

00:00:15.134 --> 00:00:19.462
and I'm here to tell you today
that people like me need your help.

00:00:20.119 --> 00:00:22.143
And in a few moments, I will tell you how.

00:00:22.167 --> 00:00:26.287
But first, let me start off by telling you
about a patient of mine.

00:00:26.311 --> 00:00:28.954
This was a woman in her 50s,

00:00:28.978 --> 00:00:31.025
she was in generally good shape,

00:00:31.049 --> 00:00:33.437
but she had been in and out
of hospital a few times

00:00:33.461 --> 00:00:36.688
due to curative breast cancer treatment.

00:00:37.768 --> 00:00:40.641
Now she had gotten a prolapse
from a cervical disc,

00:00:40.665 --> 00:00:44.339
giving her radiating pain of a tense kind,

00:00:44.363 --> 00:00:46.307
out into the right arm.

00:00:47.061 --> 00:00:49.823
Looking at her MRI
before the consultation,

00:00:49.847 --> 00:00:51.616
I decided to suggest an operation.

00:00:52.411 --> 00:00:56.852
Now, neck operations like these
are standardized, and they're quick.

00:00:57.279 --> 00:00:58.890
But they carry a certain risk.

00:00:58.914 --> 00:01:01.064
You make an incision right here,

00:01:01.088 --> 00:01:03.945
and you dissect carefully
past the trachea,

00:01:03.969 --> 00:01:05.307
the esophagus,

00:01:05.331 --> 00:01:08.568
and you try not to cut
into the internal carotid artery.

00:01:08.592 --> 00:01:09.855
(Laughter)

00:01:09.879 --> 00:01:12.117
Then you bring in the microscope,

00:01:12.141 --> 00:01:14.514
and you carefully remove
the disc and the prolapse

00:01:14.538 --> 00:01:15.728
in the nerve root canal,

00:01:15.752 --> 00:01:17.871
without damaging the cord
and the nerve root

00:01:17.895 --> 00:01:20.209
lying only millimeters underneath.

00:01:20.625 --> 00:01:23.085
The worst case scenario
is the damage to the cord,

00:01:23.109 --> 00:01:27.267
which can result in paralysis
from the neck down.

00:01:28.673 --> 00:01:31.205
Explaining this to the patient,
she fell silent.

00:01:31.229 --> 00:01:32.436
And after a few moments,

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:36.244
she uttered a few very decisive words
for me and for her.

00:01:37.887 --> 00:01:41.371
"Doctor, is this really necessary?"

00:01:41.395 --> 00:01:42.883
(Laughter)

00:01:44.101 --> 00:01:47.034
And you know what I realized,
right there and then?

00:01:47.418 --> 00:01:48.568
It was not.

00:01:49.561 --> 00:01:52.585
In fact, when I get patients
like this woman,

00:01:52.609 --> 00:01:55.085
I tend to advise not to operate.

00:01:55.665 --> 00:01:57.798
So what made me do it this time?

00:01:59.153 --> 00:02:00.805
Well, you see,

00:02:00.829 --> 00:02:03.065
this prolapse was so delicate,

00:02:03.089 --> 00:02:06.780
I could practically see myself
pulling it out of the nerve root canal

00:02:06.804 --> 00:02:09.280
before she entered the consultation room.

00:02:09.907 --> 00:02:12.907
I have to admit it,
I wanted to operate on her.

00:02:13.359 --> 00:02:15.232
I'd love to operate on her.

00:02:15.256 --> 00:02:18.873
Operating, after all,
is the most fun part of my job.

00:02:18.897 --> 00:02:21.531
(Laughter)

00:02:22.538 --> 00:02:24.728
I think you can relate to this feeling.

00:02:24.752 --> 00:02:28.633
My architect neighbor says
he loves to just sit and draw

00:02:28.657 --> 00:02:30.093
and design houses.

00:02:30.117 --> 00:02:31.490
He'd rather do that all day

00:02:31.514 --> 00:02:35.406
than talk to the client
paying for the house

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:38.374
that might even give him
restrictions on what to do.

00:02:39.073 --> 00:02:40.348
But like every architect,

00:02:40.372 --> 00:02:43.358
every surgeon needs
to look their patient in the eye

00:02:43.382 --> 00:02:44.847
and together with the patient,

00:02:44.871 --> 00:02:48.865
they need to decide on what is best
for the person having the operation.

00:02:50.264 --> 00:02:51.684
And that might sound easy.

00:02:51.708 --> 00:02:54.329
But let's look at some statistics.

00:02:56.009 --> 00:02:59.107
The tonsils are the two lumps
in the back of your throat.

00:02:59.419 --> 00:03:01.569
They can be removed surgically,

00:03:01.593 --> 00:03:03.649
and that's called a tonsillectomy.

00:03:03.673 --> 00:03:07.974
This chart shows the operation rate
of tonsillectomies in Norway

00:03:07.998 --> 00:03:09.427
in different regions.

00:03:09.451 --> 00:03:13.133
What might strike you
is that there is twice the chance

00:03:13.157 --> 00:03:16.688
that your kid --
because this is for children --

00:03:16.712 --> 00:03:20.250
will get a tonsillectomy in Finnmark
than in Trondheim.

00:03:20.862 --> 00:03:23.613
The indications
in both regions are the same.

00:03:24.196 --> 00:03:26.452
There should be
no difference, but there is.

00:03:26.759 --> 00:03:28.211
Here's another chart.

00:03:28.235 --> 00:03:30.403
The meniscus helps stabilize the knee

00:03:30.427 --> 00:03:32.683
and can be torn or fragmented acutely,

00:03:32.707 --> 00:03:35.107
topically during sports like soccer.

00:03:35.625 --> 00:03:38.861
What you see here is the operation rate
for this condition.

00:03:39.354 --> 00:03:43.125
And you see that the operation
rate in Møre og Romsdal

00:03:43.149 --> 00:03:46.834
is five times the operation
rate in Stavanger.

00:03:47.427 --> 00:03:48.775
Five times.

00:03:49.133 --> 00:03:50.418
How can this be?

00:03:50.442 --> 00:03:52.424
Did the soccer players in Møre og Romsdal

00:03:52.448 --> 00:03:54.599
play more dirty
than elsewhere in the country?

00:03:54.623 --> 00:03:56.265
(Laughter)

00:03:56.815 --> 00:03:57.965
Probably not.

00:03:59.109 --> 00:04:01.268
I added some information now.

00:04:01.292 --> 00:04:03.442
What you see now
is the procedures performed

00:04:03.466 --> 00:04:05.498
in public hospitals, in light blue,

00:04:05.522 --> 00:04:08.506
the ones in private clinics
are light green.

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:11.880
There is a lot of activity
in the private clinics

00:04:11.904 --> 00:04:13.837
in Møre og Romsdal, isn't there?

00:04:14.363 --> 00:04:15.736
What does this indicate?

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:19.696
A possible economic motivation
to treat the patients.

00:04:20.522 --> 00:04:21.672
And there's more.

00:04:23.577 --> 00:04:27.340
Recent research has shown
that the difference of treatment effect

00:04:27.364 --> 00:04:30.815
between regular physical therapy
and operations for the knee --

00:04:30.839 --> 00:04:32.363
there is no difference.

00:04:32.387 --> 00:04:36.142
Meaning that most
of the procedures performed

00:04:36.166 --> 00:04:38.372
on the chart I've just shown

00:04:38.396 --> 00:04:41.047
could have been avoided,
even in Stavanger.

00:04:41.785 --> 00:04:44.222
So what am I trying to tell you here?

00:04:44.246 --> 00:04:49.371
Even though most indications
for treatments in the world

00:04:49.395 --> 00:04:50.561
are standardized,

00:04:50.585 --> 00:04:54.879
there is a lot of unnecessary variation
of treatment decisions,

00:04:54.903 --> 00:04:56.736
especially in the Western world.

00:04:57.572 --> 00:05:00.748
Some people are not getting
the treatment that they need,

00:05:01.493 --> 00:05:04.556
but an even greater portion of you

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:06.167
are being overtreated.

00:05:08.649 --> 00:05:11.434
"Doctor, is this really necessary?"

00:05:11.458 --> 00:05:14.450
I've only heard that question
once in my career.

00:05:14.474 --> 00:05:18.332
My colleagues say they never heard
these words from a patient.

00:05:19.431 --> 00:05:21.185
And to turn it the other way around,

00:05:21.209 --> 00:05:23.764
how often do you think
you'll get a "no" from a doctor

00:05:23.788 --> 00:05:25.588
if you ask such a question?

00:05:26.487 --> 00:05:28.196
Researchers have investigated this,

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:30.973
and they come up
with about the same "no" rate

00:05:30.997 --> 00:05:32.164
wherever they go.

00:05:32.188 --> 00:05:33.895
And that is 30 percent.

00:05:33.919 --> 00:05:37.886
Meaning, three out of 10 times,

00:05:37.910 --> 00:05:41.449
your doctor prescribes
or suggests something

00:05:41.473 --> 00:05:44.147
that is completely unnecessary.

00:05:46.545 --> 00:05:49.434
And you know what they claim
the reason for this is?

00:05:50.117 --> 00:05:51.267
Patient pressure.

00:05:52.832 --> 00:05:54.165
In other words, you.

00:05:54.943 --> 00:05:56.943
You want something to be done.

00:05:58.665 --> 00:06:01.061
A friend of mine came to me
for medical advice.

00:06:01.085 --> 00:06:02.237
This is a sporty guy,

00:06:02.261 --> 00:06:05.815
he does a lot of cross-country skiing
in the winter time,

00:06:05.839 --> 00:06:07.545
he runs in the summer time.

00:06:07.569 --> 00:06:11.569
And this time, he'd gotten a bad back ache
whenever he went jogging.

00:06:11.593 --> 00:06:13.786
So much that he had to stop doing it.

00:06:15.053 --> 00:06:18.357
I did an examination,
I questioned him thoroughly,

00:06:18.381 --> 00:06:21.515
and what I found out is
that he probably had a degenerated disc

00:06:21.539 --> 00:06:23.606
in the lower part of his spine.

00:06:24.087 --> 00:06:26.450
Whenever it got strained, it hurt.

00:06:28.165 --> 00:06:30.513
He'd already taken up
swimming instead of jogging,

00:06:30.537 --> 00:06:32.038
there was really nothing to do,

00:06:32.062 --> 00:06:34.196
so I told him, "You need
to be more selective

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:35.537
when it comes to training.

00:06:35.561 --> 00:06:37.164
Some activities are good for you,

00:06:37.188 --> 00:06:38.418
some are not."

00:06:39.665 --> 00:06:40.957
His reply was,

00:06:41.919 --> 00:06:43.719
"I want an MRI of my back."

00:06:45.776 --> 00:06:47.155
"Why do you want an MRI?"

00:06:48.236 --> 00:06:51.036
"I can get it for free
through my insurance at work."

00:06:51.807 --> 00:06:54.926
"Come on," I said --
he was also, after all, my friend.

00:06:54.950 --> 00:06:56.832
"That's not the real reason."

00:06:56.856 --> 00:07:01.356
"Well, I think it's going to be good
to see how bad it looks back there."

00:07:03.094 --> 00:07:06.039
"When did you start interpreting
MRI scans?" I said.

00:07:06.063 --> 00:07:08.113
(Laughter)

00:07:08.137 --> 00:07:09.589
"Trust me on this.

00:07:10.026 --> 00:07:12.086
You're not going to need the scan."

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:14.533
"Well," he said,

00:07:14.557 --> 00:07:17.478
and after a while, he continued,
"It could be cancer."

00:07:17.869 --> 00:07:20.073
(Laughter)

00:07:20.097 --> 00:07:21.897
He got the scan, obviously.

00:07:22.907 --> 00:07:24.541
And through his insurance at work,

00:07:24.565 --> 00:07:26.605
he got to see one
of my colleagues at work,

00:07:26.629 --> 00:07:28.756
telling him about the degenerated disc,

00:07:28.780 --> 00:07:30.196
that there was nothing to do,

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:33.605
and that he should keep on swimming
and quit the jogging.

00:07:36.196 --> 00:07:38.260
After a while,
I met him again and he said,

00:07:38.284 --> 00:07:40.237
"At least now I know what this is."

00:07:40.712 --> 00:07:42.331
But let me ask you a question.

00:07:42.355 --> 00:07:46.474
What if all of you in this room
with the same symptoms had an MRI?

00:07:46.498 --> 00:07:49.188
And what if all the people in Norway

00:07:49.212 --> 00:07:52.822
had an MRI due to occasional back pain?

00:07:54.061 --> 00:07:57.673
The waiting list for an MRI
would quadruple, maybe even more.

00:07:58.180 --> 00:08:00.945
And you would all take
the spot on that list

00:08:00.969 --> 00:08:03.302
from someone who really had cancer.

00:08:03.919 --> 00:08:07.491
So a good doctor sometimes says no,

00:08:07.515 --> 00:08:11.864
but the sensible patient
also turns down, sometimes,

00:08:11.888 --> 00:08:14.542
an opportunity
to get diagnosed or treated.

00:08:16.782 --> 00:08:20.083
"Doctor, is this really necessary?"

00:08:20.472 --> 00:08:23.789
I know this can be
a difficult question to ask.

00:08:24.377 --> 00:08:26.276
In fact, if you go back 50 years,

00:08:26.300 --> 00:08:28.653
this was even considered rude.

00:08:28.677 --> 00:08:29.799
(Laughter)

00:08:29.823 --> 00:08:32.269
If the doctor had decided
what to do with you,

00:08:32.293 --> 00:08:33.627
that's what you did.

00:08:35.574 --> 00:08:37.819
A colleague of mine,
now a general practitioner,

00:08:37.843 --> 00:08:42.184
was sent away to a tuberculosis
sanatorium as a little girl,

00:08:42.208 --> 00:08:43.367
for six months.

00:08:43.391 --> 00:08:45.248
It was a terrible trauma for her.

00:08:45.703 --> 00:08:48.044
She later found out, as a grown-up,

00:08:48.068 --> 00:08:51.609
that her tests on tuberculosis
had been negative all along.

00:08:52.403 --> 00:08:55.942
The doctor had sent her away
on nothing but wrong suspicion.

00:08:56.419 --> 00:09:00.823
No one had dared or even considered
confronting him about it.

00:09:01.268 --> 00:09:02.668
Not even her parents.

00:09:03.506 --> 00:09:06.752
Today, the Norwegian health minister

00:09:06.776 --> 00:09:10.156
talks about the patient
health care service.

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:15.086
The patient is supposed to get advice
from the doctor about what to do.

00:09:16.340 --> 00:09:18.181
This is great progress.

00:09:18.205 --> 00:09:21.586
But it also puts more
responsibility on you.

00:09:21.919 --> 00:09:24.836
You need to get in the front seat
with your doctor

00:09:24.860 --> 00:09:27.007
and start sharing
decisions on where to go.

00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:30.983
So, the next time
you're in a doctor's office,

00:09:31.007 --> 00:09:32.896
I want you to ask,

00:09:32.920 --> 00:09:35.983
"Doctor, is this really necessary?"

00:09:36.315 --> 00:09:38.624
And in my female patient's case,

00:09:38.648 --> 00:09:40.958
the answer would be no,

00:09:40.982 --> 00:09:43.649
but an operation could also be justified.

00:09:44.165 --> 00:09:48.886
"So doctors, what are the risks
attached to this operation?"

00:09:49.488 --> 00:09:54.942
Well, five to ten percent of patients
will have worsening of pain symptoms.

00:09:55.427 --> 00:09:57.141
One to two percent of patients

00:09:57.165 --> 00:10:00.180
will have an infection in the wound
or even a rehemorrhage

00:10:00.204 --> 00:10:02.604
that might end up in a re-operation.

00:10:04.069 --> 00:10:07.656
0.5 percent of patients
also experience permanent hoarseness

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:09.474
and a few, but still a few,

00:10:09.498 --> 00:10:13.867
will experience reduced function
in the arms or even legs.

00:10:15.163 --> 00:10:17.615
"Doctor, are there other options?"

00:10:18.131 --> 00:10:21.509
Yes, rest and physical therapy
over some time

00:10:21.533 --> 00:10:23.559
might get you perfectly well.

00:10:25.105 --> 00:10:27.835
"And what happens if I don't do anything?"

00:10:27.859 --> 00:10:29.034
It's not recommended,

00:10:29.058 --> 00:10:32.089
but even then, there's a slight chance
that you will get well.

00:10:33.002 --> 00:10:34.152
Four questions.

00:10:34.780 --> 00:10:36.297
Simple questions.

00:10:36.321 --> 00:10:40.678
Consider them your new toolbox to help us.

00:10:40.702 --> 00:10:42.313
Is this really necessary?

00:10:42.337 --> 00:10:44.130
What are the risks?

00:10:44.154 --> 00:10:45.710
Are there other options?

00:10:45.734 --> 00:10:48.894
And what happens if I don't do anything?

00:10:49.334 --> 00:10:53.381
Ask them when your doctor
wants to send you to an MRI,

00:10:53.405 --> 00:10:55.698
when he prescribes antibiotics

00:10:55.722 --> 00:10:57.825
or suggests an operation.

00:10:58.282 --> 00:10:59.990
What we know from research

00:11:00.014 --> 00:11:03.204
is that one out of five
of you, 20 percent,

00:11:03.228 --> 00:11:05.759
will change your opinion on what to do.

00:11:06.624 --> 00:11:09.966
And by doing that, you will
not only have made your life

00:11:09.990 --> 00:11:13.552
a whole lot easier,
and probably even better,

00:11:13.576 --> 00:11:15.532
but the whole health care sector

00:11:15.556 --> 00:11:18.616
will have benefited from your decision.

00:11:19.354 --> 00:11:20.536
Thank you.

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:24.246
(Applause)

